# Server credentials — IP, port, account names and password. Never commit.
.env

# Per-agent keeper state (live runtime: character name, HP, pack, room) — one
# server's characters, rewritten by a running keeper. Same class as the sheets.
/substrate/keeper-*.json
/substrate/keeper-*.log

# The client's own art: 5,355 PNGs decoded from its .bgf files, 40 MB of it, and
# not ours to redistribute. Regenerate from any local client with
#     python tools/pull-client-assets.py
/compendium/assets/img/

node_modules/

# Everything a running fleet writes. This repository is a starting point, not a
# snapshot of somebody's characters: the roster carries account passwords in
# plain text, and the flight recordings are one server's history, not reference.
/substrate/fleet-state.json
/substrate/fleet-state.json.*
# Named fleets (--fleet <name>) keep a roster each under here. Same plaintext
# passwords as the file above, one file per server, so the whole directory goes —
# a rule naming only fleet-state.json stops covering the roster the moment anyone
# runs the very feature that directory exists for.
/substrate/fleets/
/substrate/history/
# Archived stats, moved aside so a run starts from a clean sheet. Same content as the two
# directories it is made of, and the same reason for keeping it out: it names characters and
# it is one machine's record of one fleet, not reference anybody cloning this should carry.
/substrate/archive/
/substrate/recordings/
# One JSON per death. Carries the last thirty lines the server sent that character,
# which is other players' speech as well as combat text, so it is one server's
# transcript and not reference material.
/substrate/postmortems/
/substrate/deaths.jsonl
# One JSON per character: how good it is at each skill and spell, and every change
# since it was first read. One server's characters, and the history is theirs — the
# same reason the roster and the post-mortems are not here.
/substrate/abilities/
# A BASELINE IS A MEASUREMENT OF ONE FLEET, NOT A FIXTURE. Each row carries a character
# name, an account handle and that character's skill levels on the day it was captured,
# which is the roster in a different shape — and on this server the short handles are the
# accounts themselves. The experiment's CODE and its test are reference material and are
# committed; the numbers it was measured against are this machine's.
/substrate/*-baseline.json
# One JSON per character: faction membership observed from that character's own
# player profile. This is live roster state, not reference data.
/substrate/faction-status/
# One JSON per character: what it has in the bank, written down each time a banker
# states it, because the server never sends the number again. Same reasoning as the
# abilities above — it is one server's characters and their money, not reference data.
# Worth backing up if you care about the fleet: it cannot be reconstructed without
# walking every character to a counter.
/substrate/banks/

# Vault contents, guild chest contents and the guild's rent position. Written by the
# running fleet whenever a vaultman or Frular happens to state one, exactly like banks/
# above — a record of what somebody was told, not source.
/substrate/storage/
# One JSON per character: the bio other players see when they look at it, written down
# at the moment it is sent because NOTHING READS A DESCRIPTION BACK — there is no packet
# for it, so this is the only copy. One server's characters and the words we put in their
# mouths, so not reference material; worth backing up if you care about the fleet.
/substrate/descriptions/
# One JSON per character: where it was standing each time it took damage, folded into
# segments by square. Written off the event stream rather than by the keeper, so it keeps
# recording through a multi-minute travel and through an errand holding the keeper inert —
# which is where most of this fleet's deaths happen and where nothing else is looking.
# One server's characters and their bad afternoons, so not reference data.
/substrate/hits/

# Every row names a character and says where it was stuck — same rule as the hits ledger.
/substrate/tactics/

# The fleet's own recorded walks and the doors it learned — both name characters and
# positions, and both are this machine's history rather than anything a clone should carry.
/substrate/trips/

# Every body's path through every room, ours and everyone else's. Names characters and
# real people who happened to be in the room, so it never leaves this machine.
/substrate/trails/
/substrate/m59-track-strikes.json
/substrate/m59-crossings-learned.json
# One JSON per character: every point of MAXIMUM HEALTH it has earned, and the creature
# and room that paid for it. Caught from the server's own announcement — "You suddenly
# feel a little tougher.", player.kod:144 — rather than inferred from a five-minute
# sample, which is why it can name the kill at all. Rare, precious, never rotated, and
# still one server's characters rather than reference data.
/substrate/tougher/
# One JSON per character: how long each map took to cross, per attempt, with how many exit
# squares had to be tried. TIME EXPOSED is the travel number worth having — damage on the
# road is normal in this game and is not a fault — and two minutes inside one room is a
# slow crossing rather than a dangerous map. One server's characters and their journeys.
/substrate/transits/
/substrate/strategy-stats/

# TRAVEL STRATEGIES, AND THE CODE OF THEM, NOT ONLY THEIR SETTINGS.
#
# Everything else in this file hides a NUMBER and commits the shape. This hides the
# STRATEGY, which is a step further, and it is deliberate: a travel strategy is executable
# and it is an instruction to a character. `substrate/strategies/*.mjs` are loaded and RUN
# by the keeper, so a file arriving from somebody else's afternoon is code this machine's
# characters obey — the same argument as a playbook, with the stakes of an import.
#
# The blink strategies are the first: when a jam is unthreadable, cast blink and arrive at
# the room's own teleport point; and the race, which blinks pre-emptively when the walk from
# the blink point to the exit is shorter than the median crossing. Whether either is on, and
# the timings each one is judged against, are this fleet's answers and not a fact about
# Meridian 59.
#
# The SHAPE is committed beside the directory, never inside it, because the loader
# enumerates every .mjs in there and an example in there is a strategy nobody asked for:
# substrate/strategies.example.mjs.
/substrate/strategies/

# The measured cost of the blink race, per map, per exit — how long the walk from the blink
# point to each common crossing actually takes. Measured on this server by these characters
# against this movement epoch, which is exactly the kind of number that must not be averaged
# across builds; see docs/m59-evidence.md.
/substrate/blink-timings/

# Every time `canBlinkOut` was asked, what it saw and whether blinking actually helped:
# the map, the squares being crossed, the route in hand, WHERE THE MONSTERS WERE, and the
# outcome once the character had blinked. Bodies are recorded in fixture shape and players
# are redacted to roles on the way in, so a jam worth reproducing can be lifted straight
# into tools/fixtures/ and committed as a test case — which is the point of keeping it.
# One server's characters, one machine's evidence; see substrate/strategies/.
/substrate/blink-observations/
/substrate/*.log
/substrate/*.err
# Collision geometry baked from the exact local server/client room resources.
# The committed map is a portable reference; setup generates this server-bound copy.
/substrate/m59-map.local.json
# Which process m59-service.mjs last started, per fleet. Runtime state, and wrong the
# moment it is read on any other machine.
/substrate/*.pid
# Which fleet THIS checkout means when nothing says otherwise (see m59-fleetpath.mjs).
# No secret in it — one name — but it is one machine's answer, and committing it would
# point a stranger's tools at a roster they do not have.
/substrate/fleet-default
/commissions/

# The account passwords for a fleet made by tools/m59-makefleet.mjs. This is the
# ONLY copy of them — back it up with tools/m59-backup.mjs, but never commit it.
/substrate/fleet-accounts.json

# What a restore replaced. tools/m59-restore.mjs never overwrites anything without
# first copying it aside here, which is what makes a wrong restore reversible — and
# means this directory holds ROSTERS, i.e. plaintext account passwords. It is as
# secret as the files above and must never be committed. Delete one once you are
# satisfied the restore was the one you wanted.
/substrate/.before-restore-*/

# The click-to-play shortcuts written by tools/m59-shortcuts.mjs. The client has
# no way to be handed a password except on its command line, so each of these is
# one account's credentials in plain text — as secret as the file above, and
# regenerated in a second by `node tools/setup.mjs shortcuts`.
/shortcuts/

# The server container's state: the world, the accounts, and the channel logs,
# which are real player chat. Delete to start the server over.
/docker/data/

# Transient logs from background helpers and test sweeps. Each is a record of one
# run against one server state, so none of it is worth carrying forward.
.m59-*.log
.build-m59.log

# The Meridian 59 client writes these into whatever directory it is launched from
/meridian.ini
/config.ini
/d3dlog.txt
/mail/
/ads/
/download/
/help/
/resource/

# Personal Claude Code settings — the status line points at a broker port that is
# a local choice, not a shared one.
.claude/settings.local.json

__pycache__/
*.pyc
/substrate/keeper-uptime.jsonl
/substrate/keeper-active.json

# The running handoff for a live fleet. It names characters and accounts and states the
# password convention in plain text, and this repository is public. CLAUDE.md has always
# said this file is gitignored; it was not, and only luck kept it out of a commit.
/NEXT-STEPS-fleet.md
# Supervisor and errand logs — machine state, and they quote character names.
/substrate/supervise-prod.err
/substrate/supervise-prod.log
/substrate/supervise.log

# Supervisor single-instance lock — machine state, one per broker port.
/substrate/supervise-*.pid

# THE CHARACTER SHEETS, AND THE CHECKPOINT SERIES UNDER THEM.
#
# This rule REVERSES an earlier decision, and the earlier reasoning is kept because it was
# not wrong — it was answering a question that now has a better answer.
#
# It said: substrate/sheets/<Character>.json IS committed, deliberately, because it is a
# character export for a server we do not run and cannot snapshot (see m59-sheet.mjs), and
# a backup that exists only on the machine that made it is the failure the tool was written
# to fix. Git was the off-machine copy.
#
# What changed is that the sheets are no longer only on the machine that made them:
# tools/m59-backup.mjs copies them to every configured destination, hashes them, and reads
# them back. So git is not the last line of defence any more, and what it was contributing
# instead was noise — twenty-one files that rewrite themselves every time the fleet moves
# or a backup runs `--checkpoint`, turning every `git status` during live work into a wall
# of modified sheets that has to be read past to find the real changes.
#
# THIS RULE ALONE CHANGES NOTHING FOR THE FILES ALREADY IN THE INDEX. .gitignore governs
# UNTRACKED files, so anything git is already tracking keeps being tracked and keeps
# showing as modified. Untracking them is `git rm --cached`, which is a commit that removes
# them from the tree — a separate, deliberate act, not a side effect of this line.
/substrate/sheets/
#
# The timestamped series underneath was already excluded — `--checkpoint` writes 21 more
# every run — and stays excluded by the rule above.
/substrate/sheets/checkpoints/

# ORDERS LIVE ON THE MACHINE THAT GIVES THEM. THIS RULE REVERSES AN EARLIER DECISION.
#
# It said: loadouts are committed, because a loadout is a plan rather than a credential —
# no account, no password — and it is the only record of a decision somebody made in the
# planner. That was true about SECRECY and wrong about OWNERSHIP.
#
# A loadout is not a description of a character, it is an INSTRUCTION TO ONE: what to carry,
# what floor triggers a supply trip, what to shed on sight, what to learn next. Committed,
# twenty-one of them travel to every clone and every clone's planner edits them back, so two
# machines running two rosters hand each other conflicting orders through git — and the
# merge is silent, because a loadout that parses is a loadout the keeper obeys. Nobody's
# fleet is anybody else's business, and a stranger cloning this repository should get the
# TOOLS for controlling a fleet and none of the orders for ours.
#
# The same argument, unchanged, is why substrate/policy.local.json, substrate/tuning.json
# and the playbooks below are not here. What is committed instead is the SHAPE:
# substrate/loadouts.example.json, substrate/tuning.example.json and
# substrate/guild-plan.example.json — outside the directories the tools enumerate, because
# an example INSIDE substrate/loadouts/ is a twenty-second character to listLoadouts().
#
# As with the sheets above, this line governs untracked files only; the ones already in the
# index were removed with `git rm --cached` in the commit that added this rule, which leaves
# every one of them exactly where it is on disk.
#
# writeLoadout renames a .tmp into place so a keeper never reads half a file; the .tmp
# exists for milliseconds and must never be committed.
/substrate/loadouts/
/substrate/loadouts/*.tmp

# TACTICS, CHANGED MID-SESSION. substrate/tuning.json is the live overlay read by
# tools/m59-tuning.mjs — whether to chase a flagged attacker, which weapons a named
# character prefers, the flee and rest fractions, which rooms it may be in at all. It is
# per-character ORDERS by definition and it is edited in a hurry, which is exactly the file
# nobody wants arriving from somebody else's afternoon. The shape is in tuning.example.json.
/substrate/tuning.json

# WHAT THE GUILD HALL SHOULD HOLD. substrate/guild-plan.json is an end state for chests in
# a hall this fleet bought on one server, and every character reads it on every town trip
# and holds items back from vendors to satisfy it. Committed, it withholds goods across
# fleets to fill a hall the clone does not own. Shape: guild-plan.example.json.
/substrate/guild-plan.json

# The last run of `node tools/m59-overhead.mjs` — one fleet's seconds of fighting, travel
# and trade, per agent handle. A report about one afternoon on one roster; regenerated by
# running the tool.
/substrate/overhead-last.json

# WHEN THIS OPERATOR LAST SAW THE UNDEAD SHIFT BEGIN. The graveyard cycle is arithmetic,
# but its PHASE is anchored by a person who looked at the sky on one server — see the
# header of tools/m59-dayclock.mjs. Inherited by a clone it is a confidently wrong clock,
# which is worse than none: absent, the tool says it has no anchor and asks for one.
/substrate/gy-cycle.json

# Anything under substrate/ that starts with a dot is a local before-I-changed-something
# copy — .loadouts-before-<change>/, .guild-<name>-before-disband.json, .autopilot-*.bak.
# They exist so a bad afternoon can be undone on THIS machine, and several of them are
# rosters or orders. .before-restore-*/ above is the same rule stated once already.
/substrate/.*

# PLAYBOOKS ARE NOT COMMITTED, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THEY DIFFER FROM LOADOUTS.
#
# substrate/playbooks/<character>.json says what to do when a player attacks, when the
# character dies, and when it improves. A loadout is a shopping list; this is a response
# to other people, and two things in it are not like a plan:
#
#   * `say` and `tell` carry LITERAL TEXT SENT TO STRANGERS on a shared server,
#     attributable to whoever owns the account. Committing it publishes what the fleet
#     will say, to whom, and under exactly what conditions;
#   * `logoff` publishes when the fleet runs and for how long, which is precisely the
#     thing somebody who wanted to grief it would read first.
#
# So it stays on the machine that owns the roster. If a playbook is ever worth sharing,
# share the SHAPE — doctrines in meridian59-dum-bot are written to be public and address
# characters by role.
/substrate/playbooks/

# THE GRUDGE BOOK NAMES REAL PEOPLE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS NOT COMMITTED.
#
# substrate/grudges.json is who has attacked this fleet in the last hour. Every row is a
# real player on a shared server, recorded by us, with a timestamp and a list of who they
# hit. Committing it would publish an accusation against a named stranger — permanently,
# in a public repository, from a record they cannot see or contest, and which can be wrong
# (the key is a NAME, deliberately; see the note at the top of m59-grudge.mjs).
#
# It is also operational: it says exactly which characters were where and when they were
# losing, which is the second thing somebody who wanted to grief this fleet would read.
/substrate/grudges.json
# AND ONE PER FLEET, for the same reason and with the same contents. The book is scoped by
# fleet because a grudge is about players on ONE SERVER — the shadow fleet on the local test
# server was inheriting production's enemies and refusing to stop at safe walls because of a
# fight happening somewhere its characters cannot be attacked. See m59-grudge.mjs.
/substrate/grudges-*.json

# AND THE SIGHTING BOOK NAMES THEM TOO — MORE OF THEM, AND WHEN THEY WERE NOT DOING
# ANYTHING TO US.
#
# The grudge book above records people who attacked this fleet. These four record everyone
# the fleet has ever stood next to: substrate/players-seen.json is one row per stranger
# with a last-seen room, substrate/player-history/<name>.jsonl is that person's movement
# trail over time, substrate/targets.json is an operator's list of who to fight, and
# substrate/active-conflicts.json says which of our characters is fighting whom right now.
#
# Committed, the first two publish a surveillance record of named strangers who have done
# nothing — a heatmap of where a real person plays and at what hour — and the third
# publishes a list of people we intend to attack. All of it is written by us, about people
# who cannot see or contest it, and a name is the only key (see m59-grudge.mjs).
#
# The fourth is also live operational state, and shares the grudges.json problem of saying
# exactly where our characters are while they are in a fight.
/substrate/players-seen.json
/substrate/player-history/
/substrate/targets.json
/substrate/active-conflicts.json

# Verified daily guild payments are live per-character operational state. They name the
# local fleet and characters and exist only to prevent a restart from charging twice.
/substrate/guild-tithes/

# Player observation logs — sightings of other players on the live server (name, room,
# object_id, timestamps). One server's data, not reference material, and identifying
# to the players observed.
/substrate/player-history/
/substrate/players-seen.json

# Runtime state files written by swarm agents or director processes.
/substrate/ai-director-status.json
/substrate/targets.json

# Local machine config files. .mcp.json carries the broker port (a local choice) and
# start-broker.sh carries the full server host — both are regenerated per machine.
# The initial-commit .mcp.json template uses 127.0.0.1 and is the only committed form.
start-broker.sh

# Swarm agent working artifacts — not part of the project.
PROJECT_CLOSURE.md
.claude/worktrees/
goap-cooldowns.json
goap-gear-dispatch.json
m59-nav-learned.json

# TEST-BED SCENARIOS ARE CREDENTIALS. A tools/m59-testbed.mjs spec carries an account and
# a password per character — it has to, because creating the account is the one step that
# cannot go over the game protocol — so a real scenario is as secret as a roster and stays
# on the machine that owns it. The *.example.json beside them is the exception: it exists
# to be read, and the passwords in it are placeholders that match no account anywhere.
/scenarios/*.json
!/scenarios/*.example.json

# THIS CHECKOUT'S OPINIONS, not this repository's. Vigor floors, rest and flee
# thresholds, carry limits — numbers that are a bet about one roster on one server on
# one afternoon, and that become advice to a stranger the moment they are committed.
# The committed defaults in m59-supervise.mjs stay as they are and remain what a fresh
# clone runs; this file is how a machine says something different. Absent means the
# committed behaviour, unchanged. See tools/m59-localpolicy.mjs.
/substrate/policy.local.json

# Bearer capabilities that let another machine — or another person — drive some of
# this fleet through the gateway, for a while. A grant file names who was trusted and
# with what; the token itself is never stored, only a salted hash. Gitignored for the
# same reason the roster is: it is this machine's authority, not the repository's.
# See tools/m59-handoff.mjs.
/substrate/grants/

# A GOAP GOAL FILE NAMES A REAL ROSTER, so it is this machine's plan rather than this
# repository's — the same split as substrate/policy.local.json. The committed
# goap-goals.example.json is the shape, with characters that belong to nobody; the live
# one is whichever fleet the operator actually runs.
/substrate/goap-goals.json

# WHERE THE COLLISION MODEL AND THE WORLD DISAGREE, per room and direction. Live
# observation of one server, written by a running fleet — the same reasoning as banks/
# and storage/ above. Useful to review, not reference data.
/substrate/exit-gaps.json

/substrate/traces/

# ---- live state written by the debugging tools added 2026-08-17 ----
# Each of these names a character and where it was standing, or is a book of
# objects this machine created in its own world. Same class of thing as the
# grudge book: per-machine, and an accusation or a position in every row.
/substrate/client-signals.jsonl
/substrate/safewalk.json
/substrate/pings.json
/substrate/tour-bookmark.json
/substrate/swarm-leader.json
/substrate/walks/

# The per-room overlay files the patched client reads. Derived from the baked
# map in about fifteen minutes, ~1.5 MB, and its absence is LOUD rather than
# silent — the client's own caption says "no overlay" — so unlike the routing
# table there is no argument for carrying it in git.
/substrate/overlay/
/substrate/circuit-runs.json
/substrate/hoptests.json
/substrate/hoptest-*.log
/substrate/walktrials.json
.DS_Store
.pi/

# Scratch verification scripts and one-run verification outputs — not reference.
_verify2.mjs
/substrate/phase3-health-verification.txt

# The shadow-fleet tool. Dry-runs a prod operation against a local copy of the
# roster, so it reads live production characters and writes their stats, position
# and equipment to disk. Not to be shared in the repository.
/tools/m59-shadow.mjs
/substrate/shadow-snapshot.json

# WHERE THIS FLEET COMMUTES. Orders to characters rather than a description of them, so it
# lives on the machine that owns the roster. substrate/commute.example.json carries the shape.
/substrate/commute.json
/substrate/shuttle-runs.json
/substrate/collision-trace.jsonl
/substrate/cragged-stalls.jsonl

# The fleet-run lock: which process is currently driving a fleet. Runtime state,
# one per machine, and meaningless in anybody else's checkout.
/substrate/run-*.lock
/substrate/keeper-trace.jsonl

# Generated room views: one HTML file per room, rebuilt from the map and this machine's
# ledgers by tools/m59-roomview.mjs. Derived, per-machine, and they carry character names
# out of the transit logs — so they are regenerated, never committed.
/substrate/roomviews/
# Raw jam recordings (m59-recordjam.mjs): derived, and with --names they carry character
# names. A redacted one meant as a fixture is written to tools/fixtures/ on purpose.
/substrate/jams/

# Jump-trial attempts: this machine measuring one cliff, appended to per attempt.
/substrate/jumptrials.jsonl
/substrate/circuit-laps.json
# The node walk's lap count, same argument: this machine's answer, regenerated by running it.
/substrate/node-laps.json

# The review queue is this machine's working list of rooms to go and stand in. Derived from
# the ledgers, regenerated in a second, and an opinion about where to spend the next hour
# rather than a fact about the map. See m59-review.mjs.
/substrate/review-queue*.json

# What THIS machine's watchers have already said, and to whom. The last-words rotation and
# the stuck cooldown are both "have we used this up yet" state for a fleet that only exists
# here — committing either would hand a stranger a spent rotation and an hour of silence
# from characters they have never met.
/substrate/lastwords.json
/substrate/stuckwatch-*.json

# Watcher logs. Same argument as substrate/broker-<fleet>.log: they name characters and
# quote what was said on a live server.
/substrate/logs/

# Which local port a proxy is advertising for which server. One machine's answer, written
# at start-up and stale the moment that process exits.
/substrate/proxies/

# One room's jump search: the candidates m59-jumpfinder.mjs found in it. Derived from the
# .roo in seconds, and an inventory of guesses rather than of confirmed jumps — the
# confirmed ones are declarations and live in substrate/m59-falljumps.json, committed.
/substrate/jumpfinder-*.json

# Which keeper port band each fleet claimed. This machine's answer — assigned on first use,
# stable thereafter, and meaningless in a checkout with different fleets. See
# keeperPortBaseFor in m59-broker.mjs.
/substrate/keeper-bands.json

# WHICH SIDE OF A SPLIT ROOM THE PREY WAS LAST SEEN ON. This machine's observations of
# this machine's fleet, rebuilt for free the first time a character sees its quarry, and
# meaningless to a clone with different rooms baked. See tools/m59-preyside.mjs.
/substrate/prey-sides.json

# WHICH FLEET IS CURRENTLY WORKING WHERE, so two of them do not book the same room. Per
# fleet, rebuilt every time somebody declares a conflict, and a stale copy from another
# machine's afternoon is an instruction rather than a description. See tools/m59-intel.mjs.
/substrate/active-conflicts-*.json

# LEDGERS OF WHAT THIS MACHINE'S CHARACTERS DID. Named runs, named characters, and evidence
# about one fleet on one server — the same argument as history/ and recordings/. The tools
# recreate them; git carrying them would mean a clone inherits somebody else's measurements.
/substrate/jumptest-*.json
/substrate/mule-*.json

# A COPY OF A DERIVED BOOK, TAKEN BEFORE A RE-TEST. Backups of generated state are this
# machine's safety net for one afternoon, not a fact about the map.
/substrate/*.before-*.json

# WHERE THIS MACHINE'S CHARACTERS RAN FOR COVER, and whether they got there. Evidence about
# one fleet on one set of roads, named per character and stamped with the movement epoch it
# was measured under. See tools/m59-shelter.mjs.
/substrate/shelter-runs-*.json

# Per-fleet chatter settings — whether a character will narrate its objective, room, health
# and job to whoever is standing next to it. That is a decision about a SERVER: on the local
# test server it is the point, and on a shared one it is the fleet's operations read aloud to
# strangers. So it lives per fleet, on the machine that owns the roster, and git carries the
# shape in chatter.example.json instead. m59-shadow.mjs writes the shadow one on `create`.
/substrate/chatter-*.json

# The Barloque sell circuit is an ORDER to this fleet, like tuning.json and guild-plan.json.
# The committed shape is substrate/sellrun.example.json.
/substrate/sellrun.json
/substrate/merchants/
/substrate/preferences.json

# Per-NPC merchant selling rules (stack caps, money caps) and fleet preferences are
# operating choices, gitignored. Committed shapes: substrate/merchants.example.json,
# substrate/preferences.example.json.

# One scout board per machine: what our characters saw in which room, and when. Fleet
# state, not a fact about Meridian 59 — and it names rooms our convoy is moving through.
/substrate/scout-board.json

# Atomic endpoint/account ownership records for broker and lab runtimes.
/substrate/runtime-account-leases/

# An assertion about ONE server at ONE moment; the shape is in door-states.example.json.
/substrate/door-states.local.json

